   "title": "2027: APC Youth Wing, Mummymen, Hails Tinubu’s Diplomatic Achievements Mummymen, a youth wing of the All Progressives Congress (APC), has pr",
   "url": "https://x.com/PrimeReportersn/status/2116095650597101942",
   "snippet": "2027: APC Youth Wing, Mummymen, Hails Tinubu’s Diplomatic Achievements\n\nMummymen, a youth wing of the All Progressives Congress (APC), has praised President Bola Tinubu’s diplomatic engagements, describing them as a means of creating economic opportunities for Nigerians. \n\nIn a circulating graphic, the group urged Nigerians to support the continuity of the Renewed Hope Agenda\n\nAmong the achievements cited are the commercial phase of the US$1.1 billion Green Imperative Project with Brazil, signed in March 2025, and China’s zero-tariff access for eligible Nigerian exports, which took effect in May 2026.\n\n The group also referenced reported Nigeria-China trade figures of US$18 billion in the first half of 2026 and an 80 per cent year-on-year increase in Chinese imports from Nigeria. It argued that such partnerships could benefit farmers, exporters and local businesses, while calling for sustained efforts to expand domestic production and ensure Nigerians benefit from international economic agreements.",
   "body": "2027: APC Youth Wing, Mummymen, Hails Tinubu’s Diplomatic Achievements\n\nMummymen, a youth wing of the All Progressives Congress (APC), has praised President Bola Tinubu’s diplomatic engagements, describing them as a means of creating economic opportunities for Nigerians. \n\nIn a circulating graphic, the group urged Nigerians to support the continuity of the Renewed Hope Agenda\n\nAmong the achievements cited are the commercial phase of the US$1.1 billion Green Imperative Project with Brazil, signed in March 2025, and China’s zero-tariff access for eligible Nigerian exports, which took effect in May 2026.\n\n The group also referenced reported Nigeria-China trade figures of US$18 billion in the first half of 2026 and an 80 per cent year-on-year increase in Chinese imports from Nigeria. It argued that such partnerships could benefit farmers, exporters and local businesses, while calling for sustained efforts to expand domestic production and ensure Nigerians benefit from international economic agreements.",
